why: §4b says kyo is 3.3x ahead at fork/join 10k "because its fibers
  are not threads". The operator asked: okay has schedulers of its
  own too — Schedulers.forkJoin (a pool) on the JVM, Async.Drive on
  JS (a fiber is a continuation walked by a loop, no thread at all).
  So which part of the 3.3x is the thread, and which is the rest?

what: Schedulers.drive(pool) — the JS shape on the JVM: fork =
  pool.execute(Drive(prog)), join = Promise.onComplete. One lane,
  forkJoin10k_okayDrive, beside okay (loom 2712), okayPool (2300),
  rawLoom (2632), kyo (830).

predict: pool 2300 -> drive ~1500 if the per-fiber cost is the
  CompletableFuture + ForkJoinTask completion path; drive >= pool
  means the cost is the submit + the 10k sequential joins on the
  platform thread (Slot park per not-yet-done fiber), and closing the
  gap needs a structured parallel (one counter, one wake) rather than
  a cheaper fiber. Disqualifying: drive within 10% of pool.
